		"nodes": {
			"1": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "LINEAR\nThe Linear node generates a collection of linear values based on it’s min and max inlets. \nThe size of the collection is based on its size inlet, note that this inlet requires a signal at attribute flow.\n\nBy default the Linear will generate a collection of float values, but it can also generate integers, float2, float3 or float4 collections.\nThis behavior can be editted in the node panel.\n\nIn the node panel you can also choose to include(default) or exclude the max value.\nThis can be usefull for when you are using the Linear to modulate the phase offset of an oscillator as 0 and 1 would have the same result.\n\nThe Linear node can operate at Signal (default), Event or Attribute flow.\nThis can also be changed in the node panel.\n\nThe Linear node will often be your friend when working with mutiple modulation sources as we will now demonstrate in the examples below."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 287.48736572265625,
					"width": 790.1807861328125,
					"x": 56.22470474243164062,
