			"45": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 4:  OPTIMIZATION\n\nThis example is nearly the same as the previous, with one exception: the flow of the Linear nodes.\nHere we changed the flow from signal to attribute in the node panel.\n\nThis is something you can do to optimize the speed of the patch.\nAt signal flow, the collection created by the Linear node is recalculated every frame.\nThis allows you to modulate the min and max inlets and create dynamically changing collections. \n\nBut what if I never want to change the collection again? Then I am wasting computing power by recalculating the same collection over and over again.\nIn this case it can be beneficial to switch to attribute flow.\nIf you are just beginning with Wire you should not worry about this at all and just patch away and experiment to your heart's content."
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 832.6885986328125,
					"width": 1432.444091796875,
					"x": 56.605560302734375,
					"y": 1904.1282958984375
